  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🍨 Dessert Business Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Names for a dessert parlour, a dessert van or a treats business on Instagram."
    },
    "intro": "A good dessert business name sounds like a treat: a pun like Sundae Best for a fun parlour, or something soft and French for a dessert bar. Sundaes, waffles, cheesecake or dessert cups by delivery: this **dessert business name generator** has names in eight styles, from <em>Sundae Best</em> to French luxury, plus names built from your town or your own name.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Eight good dessert business names",
        "content": "<p>All eight came from the generator:</p><ul><li><strong>Sundae Best</strong>: a pun on Sunday best, for a sundae parlour.</li><li><strong>Custard's Last Stand</strong>: a pun on Custer's Last Stand, for a place that never runs out.</li><li><strong>Never on a Sundae</strong>: a joke that works on a sign.</li><li><strong>Say Cheesecake Dessert Bar</strong>: a pun built around one dessert.</li><li><strong>Cloud Cups</strong>: aesthetic and simple, for dessert cups by delivery.</li><li><strong>Wobble</strong>: one funny, modern word that suggests jelly and panna cotta.</li><li><strong>Maison Chantilly</strong>: luxury: Chantilly cream is sweetened whipped cream, named after the town of Chantilly in France.</li><li><strong>Hampton Waffle & Sundae Bar</strong>: your town plus your menu.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "How dessert business names work",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Parlours and bars want fun.</strong> Families and teenagers choose dessert shops for a treat, so puns and playful names work well.</li><li><strong>French names suggest patisserie.</strong> <em>Maison</em> (house), <em>délices</em> (delights) and <em>sucré</em> (sweet) set expectations for something refined, and for prices to match.</li><li><strong>Delivery brands need clarity.</strong> On a delivery app, customers scroll fast, so a name that says what you sell (<em>Brownie Dessert Studio</em>) beats a clever one.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Check it's free before you print the sign",
        "content": "<p>A name from a generator is a starting point, not permission to use it. Before you order the sign, the boxes or the van livery:</p><ul><li><strong>Company register.</strong> In the UK, search Companies House; you can't register a limited company with the same name as an existing one. In the US, search your state's business register.</li><li><strong>Trade marks.</strong> Search the UK trade mark register (or the USPTO in the US) for the goods and services you'll sell.</li><li><strong>Domain and handles.</strong> Check the .co.uk or .com and the Instagram and TikTok handles you'll need.</li><li><strong>Your area.</strong> Search a map app for businesses with the same or a similar name nearby.</li></ul><p>Press <strong>Check</strong> on any name, or use the <a href=\"../business-name-checker.html\">Business Name Checker</a>, to run most of these at once. For the full process, read our guide, <a href=\"../guides/business-naming-best-practices.html\">Business Naming Best Practices</a>. This is general guidance, not legal advice.</p><p>In the UK, a dessert shop, van or delivery kitchen must register with the local council as a food business at least 28 days before trading.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Making the name work on a delivery app",
